\section{Introduction}

% ------------------- Research Background -------------------
The rapid advancement of deep learning has transformed a wide spectrum of scientific and engineering domains, ranging from computer vision and natural language processing to drug discovery and materials design \cite{lecun2015deep, silver2016mastering}.  In particular, the prediction of molecular properties—such as solubility, toxicity, and binding affinity—has become a cornerstone for accelerating the development of new therapeutics and functional materials \cite{schneider2018machine, gomez2018automatic}.  Despite the growing availability of large‐scale molecular datasets, the central challenge remains the extraction of expressive representations that faithfully capture both the discrete graph topology of molecules and the continuous physicochemical interactions among atoms.

Existing approaches to molecular property prediction can be broadly grouped into three research lines.  The first line relies on handcrafted molecular descriptors combined with conventional machine learning models (e.g., random forests or support vector machines) \cite{rogers2010extended, wu2018moleculenet}.  The second line adopts end‑to‑end deep neural networks that operate on fixed‑size fingerprints or SMILES strings, thereby bypassing manual feature engineering \cite{duvenaud2015convolutional, kearnes2016molecular}.  The third line leverages graph neural networks (GNNs) to directly process the molecular graph, propagating information along edges to generate node and graph embeddings \cite{gilmer2017neural, hamilton2017inductive}.  While GNN‑based methods have demonstrated superior performance over the first two lines, they still exhibit notable limitations in scalability, expressiveness, and interpretability.

% ------------------- Research Motivation -------------------
A critical gap in current GNN methodologies is their inability to capture long‑range chemical interactions without incurring prohibitive computational costs \cite{xu2019powerful}.  For example, standard message‑passing schemes typically restrict the receptive field to a few hops, which may overlook important non‑local effects such as aromatic ring conjugation or distant hydrogen bonding.  Moreover, many existing models treat all edges uniformly, ignoring the diverse bond types and electronic environments that differentiate chemical behavior \cite{battaglia2018relational}.  These shortcomings raise several research questions: (i) how can a model efficiently aggregate information from arbitrarily distant atoms; (ii) how can it incorporate rich edge semantics while preserving computational tractability; and (iii) how can the learned representations be made more interpretable for domain experts.

Addressing these questions demands a novel architectural paradigm that transcends the conventional message‑passing framework.  By integrating hierarchical attention mechanisms with multi‑scale graph pooling, it becomes possible to dynamically adjust the receptive field, emphasize chemically salient relationships, and produce coarse‑grained summaries that retain interpretability.  This insight motivates the development of a new graph transformer architecture tailored to molecular property prediction.

% ------------------- Methodology Overview -------------------
The proposed approach introduces a Hierarchical Attention Graph Transformer (HAGT) that operates on the molecular graph in a multi‑level fashion.  At the finest level, node embeddings are updated by self‑attention over their immediate neighborhoods, where attention coefficients are modulated by edge‑type embeddings that encode bond order, aromaticity, and other chemical attributes.  Subsequently, a learnable pooling operator clusters nodes into chemically meaningful substructures (e.g., functional groups), allowing the model to attend over these higher‑level entities in the next hierarchy.  This process repeats, yielding a series of progressively coarser graphs that capture both local and global chemical contexts.  By virtue of its hierarchical design, HAGT can aggregate information from distant atoms without resorting to deep, computationally expensive message‑passing stacks, and the attention weights provide an intrinsic mechanism for interpreting which substructures drive the predictions.

% ------------------- Contributions -------------------
\begin{itemize}
    \item We formulate a novel hierarchical graph transformer that integrates edge‑aware self‑attention with adaptive multi‑scale pooling, enabling efficient modeling of long‑range molecular interactions.
    \item We introduce a chemically informed edge embedding scheme that differentiates bond types and electronic environments, enhancing the expressive power of the attention mechanism.
    \item We provide a comprehensive empirical evaluation on benchmark molecular property datasets, demonstrating state‑of‑the‑art performance while substantially reducing computational overhead compared to existing GNNs.
    \item We present qualitative analyses showing that the learned attention maps align with known chemical intuition, thereby offering improved interpretability for downstream scientific applications.
\end{itemize}